# Recovery: error codes and what to do

Every failure carries a stable symbolic code and exit code (`error.code`, `error.exit_code`
in JSON), a message, optional `details` and often a `hint`. Read the hint first. Command:
`{{kb}}`. Never work around an error by switching to `--offline`, another snapshot
or a smaller scope without telling the user. `kb` never builds or downloads an engine
implicitly: getting a runtime is an explicit action.

Reuse the task's existing authorization. Routine reads, validation, fetches and a local
bootstrap needed for an authorized implementation/setup do not need another permission
question. Acceptance, publication, paid jobs and discarding edits need authority matching
their concrete effect. A recovery suggestion does not grant it.

| code | exit | what it means | what to do |
|---|---|---|---|
| `INTERNAL` | 1 | engine bug | Report it with the command and output; continue without claiming complete context. |
| `USAGE` | 2 | invalid or unknown arguments | Fix the command line (`{{kb}} <command> --help`). |
| `PROJECT_NOT_INITIALIZED` | 10 | the KB has no `project/project.toml` | Tell the user; initializing a KB (`kb init`) is a maintainer decision, not yours. |
| `CONFIG_INVALID` | 11 | project, skill or lock configuration is invalid | Report the message; configuration changes go through KB review. |
| `UNSUPPORTED_SCHEMA_VERSION` | 13 | a file uses a schema this engine cannot read | Stop; context is not complete. The KB needs `kb migrate` or an engine update (maintainers). |
| `ALREADY_INITIALIZED` | 14 | `init` on an initialized KB | Nothing to do; edit `project/` instead. |
| `UNKNOWN_SCOPE` | 15 | an explicit repo/module/feature/concept id does not exist | Check the ids in the KB registry (`project/registry/*.toml`) and retry. |
| `NOT_FOUND` | 16 | record, section, file, symbol or text not found (agent commands still print their answer) | For a record, check the id (`kb search <words>{{snapshot_arg}}`); record ids are stable and never reused. For code, the answer says what was covered: do not repeat the same question. A name that does not occur was looked for as a whole word, as written: check the spelling (`kb find` matches parts of words and ignores case for lower-case text), then report it, like a text or file that occurs nowhere, as absent from the files checked. ``no line writes `Type.member` `` is not final: look the member up by its own name, as `next:` says. For a name that is used while no line reads as its declaration, cite the listed uses and report what the scan saw (the declaration may be in a dependency, in generated code or in a form the scan does not recognise). |
| `FRESHNESS_UNVERIFIED` | 20 | the approved ref could not be fetched | Context is not verified. Tell the user (network, VPN, credentials). Use `--offline` only if the user accepts unverified knowledge, and report `freshness = unverified`. |
| `UPDATE_REQUIRED` | 21 | the selected snapshot needs another engine, or the host pin differs from the approved tip | Report both revisions from `details`. Ask the user to update the KB submodule through review, or to choose `--snapshot pinned` explicitly. |
| `SNAPSHOT_NOT_FOUND` | 22 | the requested revision does not exist locally or remotely | Check the revision; `{{kb}} sync` is a read-only fetch and may already be within the task's authority. |
| `SKILL_OUTDATED` | 23 | this skill is older than the engine | Re-read `SKILL.md`; if the protocol number did not change, ask the user to start a new session. |
| `CONTEXT_INCOMPLETE` | 30 | completeness is `partial`, `conflict` or `incomplete` (full result printed) | Use what was delivered, but list the undetermined, conflicting or missing parts; narrow the scope with `--path`/`--repo` when that is the cause. Never call it complete. |
| `CONTEXT_BUDGET_EXCEEDED` | 31 | mandatory knowledge does not fit the budget | Re-run with `--budget` at least the required amount from `details`; do not proceed on partial obligations. |
| `VALIDATION_FAILED` | 40 | validation or a blocking declarative probe failed | Read the diagnostics/evidence. Fix the authorized source or proposal; do not weaken a rule just to clear its probe. |
| `ROUTING_TESTS_FAILED` | 41 | golden routing cases no longer hold | Report which case failed; fix the records or the case in the KB change. |
| `DRIFT_DETECTED` | 42 | generated files or source evidence differ | For integration drift, regenerate the KB bundle then reinstall within scope. For anchor drift, inspect the changed source and review the record; do not blindly restamp it. |
| `ENGINE_DIVERGED` | 43 | engine-owned paths differ from the upstream base | Maintainers only: move engine changes upstream or record them in `project/upstream.toml`. |
| `IMPACT_UNACKNOWLEDGED` | 44 | affected knowledge or unknown coverage is not acknowledged | Update the `kb-impact` block of the merge request (`none` with a reason, `linked`, or `included`) and, when knowledge changes, propose it. |
| `CONFLICT` | 45 | generated files, managed blocks or draft identities conflict | Preserve local edits and inspect duplicates. `--force` requires existing or new explicit authority to discard those exact edits. |
| `CHECKS_FAILED` | 46 | a configured check, changed-knowledge validation or a declared probe failed (`kb check`; result printed) | Read the output lines of the failed check, fix the cause and re-run `{{kb}} check`; never edit `tools.toml` to silence a check. |
| `RUNTIME_INCOMPATIBLE` | 50 | the running binary does not match the local engine | Run plain `{{kb}}` (not a runtime binary directly); `{{kb}} engine info` shows which runtime serves the checkout. If it persists, report it. |
| `UPDATE_CONFLICT` | 51 | an upstream update could not be prepared cleanly | Maintainers resolve it in the update worktree; nothing in the main checkout changed. |
| `MIGRATION_FAILED` | 52 | a schema migration failed | Report the message; the previous files are intact. |
| `UPDATE_FAILED` | 53 | preparing an update failed | Report the message; the previous runtime and checkout are intact. |
| `RUNTIME_NOT_BOOTSTRAPPED` | 54 | no runtime exists for the checkout's engine (first use, an engine edit, or the KB checkout moved to another engine version) | Run `{{kb}} engine install` (release runtime) or `{{kb}} engine bootstrap` (source build) when that is covered by the task; otherwise explain which one is needed before asking. |
| `BOOTSTRAP_FAILED` | 55 | the source build failed; any earlier runtime is intact | Report the build output; do not retry in a loop. |
| `TOOLCHAIN_MISMATCH` | 56 | the installed rustc is not the pinned toolchain | Report it; installing toolchains is the user's decision. |
| `ARTIFACT_INVALID` | 57 | a release archive failed verification (digest, entries, BUILD-INFO, smoke test) | Report it; never bypass digest verification. |
| `PACKAGE_FAILED` | 58 | `kb engine package` could not build or write an archive | Report it (maintainers only). |
| `GIT_ERROR` | 60 | a Git command failed | Report the (redacted) message; check that you are inside the host repository. |
| `INDEX_ERROR` | 61 | the local index cache failed | Retry once; then `{{kb}} index --rebuild` (the cache is derived data). |
| `IO_ERROR` | 62 | a file could not be read or written | Report the path and message. |
| `UNSAFE_PATH` | 63 | a path escapes its root, uses `..` or a symlink | Use repository-relative paths without `..`; symlinks are refused by design. |
| `INVALID_INPUT` | 64 | an argument or file is malformed | Fix the input named in the message (for example malformed `<!-- kb:begin/end -->` markers). |
| `TOOL_UNAVAILABLE` | 65 | no connected tool could answer (not installed, no index, or every routed tool failed; `details.attempts` says why) | Run `{{kb}} tools`; report what is missing (installing tools or creating indexes is the user's decision). Read source directly for this question meanwhile. |
| `DOWNLOAD_FAILED` | 66 | a release file could not be downloaded (no network, no `curl`, or no such release) | Report it; do not switch to unverified downloads. |
